WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.209 --> 00:00:01.710
♪♪
(Vocalizing)

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:04.588
Pete Townshend:
I could see this new
revolution coming. 

00:00:04.588 --> 00:00:09.426
Pete Townshend:
It was an anticipation of the
idea that everything would go wrong,

00:00:09.426 --> 00:00:12.054
Pete Townshend:
but that music would prevail.

00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:13.263
♪Mother mother♪

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:19.186
Jimmy Iovine:
1971. I don't think the music
was a reflection of the times as much as

00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:21.772
the music  also caused the
times.

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:23.357
♪♪Brother brother brother♪

00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:25.901
Phillip Zimbardo:
It was an incredibly tumultuous
year...

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:27.110
♪♪

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:31.114
Phillip Zimbardo:
Politically, socially,
musically.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.661
I think that music reflects the
stage that the society's in.

00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:40.666
Chrissy Hynde:
All these bands were making
music referring to protests and the Vietnam war.

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:41.833
Chrissy Hynde:
That was our language.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:45.295
I would to like to say something
that hasn't been said so much,
you know.

00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:48.006
(Protest chants)

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:52.636
Peter Tatchell:
1971 was a year of revolution 
and consciousness.

00:00:52.636 --> 00:00:54.554
♪Oh, what's going on♪
♪(What's going on)♪

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:58.642
Chrissy Hynde:
We were fueled by this amazing
music that everyone was making.

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:00.143
♪I'll tell you, what's going on♪

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:03.021
Chrissy Hynde:
It was articulating everything
you're seeing and feeling.

00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:05.107
♪♪
(Vocalizing)

00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:09.778
You know, we have survived
everything that's gone down and
come out stronger.

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:11.780
♪♪
(Vocalizing)

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.783
Marvin Gaye:
I think this is my cross to
bare.

00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:15.993
Marvin Gaye:
I'm the messenger.

00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:20.747
Graham Nash:
We thought that music could
change the world.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:24.042
♪♪

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:29.047
I don't think you really really
have a new thing until you've got
new music.

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:31.091
♪We'll be fighting in the
streets♪

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.803
Robert Greenfield:
The peace and flowers, love
thing, was over.

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:38.265
Brian Jackson:
I wasn't a soldier, I was a
musician.

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:42.060
Brian Jackson:
How could they use my music to
help change things?

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.687
All power to the people.

00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:44.813
♪I'll tip my hat to the new
constitution♪

00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:49.067
Elton John:
I don't think you'll ever see a
creative burst like that musically, ever again.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.988
Robert Greenfield:
These were not just players,
they were cultural icons.

00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:56.366
We had come through something.
If we could this far, we could get
further.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:59.244
Tony Visconti:
They took this on and things
changed.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:01.246
♪♪

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:02.247
Man:
The music...

00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:03.248
♪♪

00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:05.459
Man:
Was not a soundtrack.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:06.710
♪♪

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.378
Man:
It was much more than that.

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:15.380
♪♪
(Vocalizing)

